          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What came to be called
            %em Dedekind cuts
            (a notion due to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Dedekind1872"} Dedekind (1872)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            is that which can be approximated (in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+value"} absolute value
            metric) by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Dedekind1872"} Dedekind 1872
            pointed out that a real number may be uniquely determined by its order relationships with rational numbers. That is, the real number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is determined by its
            %strong lower set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_x
            and its
            %strong upper set
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_x
          #eq:cuts.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi L
                            %mi x
                          %mo ≔
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi ℚ
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi a
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo ,
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mi x
                          %mo ≔
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi ℚ
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi x
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin {split}
                  L_x \coloneqq \{ a\colon \mathbb{Q} \;|\; a \lt x \} ,\\
                  U_x \coloneqq \{ b\colon \mathbb{Q} \;|\; x \lt b \}
                  \.\end {split}
          %p
            Dedekind had found great success understanding the ‘ideal numbers’ of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+theory"} ring theory
            as certain sets, which are what we now understand as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal+of+a+ring"} ideals
          %h2#Definitions Definitions
          %p
            Exactly which pairs of sets of rational numbers can appear this way? We will define a
            %strong Dedekind cut
            to be any pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L,U)
            of sets of rational numbers satisfying these conditions:
          %ol
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %li
              Similarly,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is inhabited.
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is a
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lower+set"} lower set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
              are rational numbers with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
