          %h2#motivation Motivation
          %p
            The point of condition (7) is that we can estimate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            as closely as we like by choosing appropriate rational numbers.
          %p
            This is entirely
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_0 \lt x
            for some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_0
            (by 1) and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo &lt;
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt b_0
            for some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_0
            (by 2), so if we wish to estimate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to within a positive rational number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            be
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌈
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌉
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lceil{2/\epsilon}\rceil
            and apply (7) to each of the finitely many (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌈
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 0
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌉
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lceil{n(b_0-a_0)}\rceil
            ) rational numbers with denominator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            that lie between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_0
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            such that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi n
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i-1)/n \lt x \lt (i+1)/n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            within
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2/n \leq \epsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            digits after a decimal point (with an uncertainty of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            in the last digit), apply (7) to each of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌈
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mn 0
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mn 10
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌉
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lceil{(b_0-a_0) \times 10^n}\rceil
            numbers between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_0
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_0
            that have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            digits after the decimal point. (We can never constructively determine the final digit with perfect certainty; for example, if we successively estimate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0.5
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0.50
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0.500
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0.5, 0.50, 0.500, \ldots
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            won't come out to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0.4999
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mn 9998
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0.4999\cdots9998
            when we go one digit farther.)
          %p
            Besides giving us a place to begin our estimation, (1&amp;2) ensure that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is finite. Condition (8) enforces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+relation"} transitivity
            law
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt x \lt b \implies a \lt b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            happens to be a rational number, we are using the sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_x
            given in
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:cuts"} (1)
            instead of their closures (defined with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            instead of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo &lt;
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            We define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt y
            to mean that there exists a rational number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            such that
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt a \lt y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and the lower set of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &lt;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
            is a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+order"} linear order
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymmetric+relation"}> Asymmetry
              \: If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt y
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \lt x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt a \lt y
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi b
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \lt b \lt x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \lt a
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comparison+relation"}> Comparison
              \: If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt z
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              is a Dedekind cut, then pick
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt a \lt z
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi b
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt a \lt b \lt z
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt y
              (if
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \lt z
              (if
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \lt b
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+relation"}> Connectedness
              \: Suppose that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt y
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \lt x
              both fail, and suppose that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b \lt y
              for some
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt x
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt b
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt b \lt y
              contradicts our assumptions, we have
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt x
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi y
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt y \implies a \lt x
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt x \implies a \lt y
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi b
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \lt x \implies b \lt y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi b
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \lt y \implies b \lt x
              similarly. Therefore,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              are equal (as pairs of subsets).
          %p
            As usual with a linear order, we define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y
            to mean that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \lt x
            is false. In particular, connectedness of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &lt;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
            corresponds to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antisymmetric+relation"} antisymmetry
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
          %p
            We can also derive a Dedekind cut from any rational number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            by taking
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:cuts"} (1)
            to be the definition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            as a Dedekind cut. Thus every rational number is interpreted as a real number. This is a strict inclusion of linear orders:
          %ul
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt y
              as rational numbers, then let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              be the rational number
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x+y)/2
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt a \lt y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \lt y
              as real numbers.
          %p
            In this way, the Dedekind cuts form an extension of the linearly ordered set of rational numbers. More than that, this extension is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+completion"} Dedekind-complete
          %p
            To be explicit, suppose that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L,U)
            is a pair of sets of Dedekind cuts (rather than of rational numbers) that satisfies (1–8), using Dedekind cuts in place of rational numbers. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sup
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sup L
            be the union of the left halves of the cuts that appear in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi inf
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \inf U
            be the union of the right halves of the cuts that appear in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi sup
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi inf
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \coloneqq (\sup L, \inf U)
            is a Dedekind cut (of rational numbers), and furthermore that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            may be recovered from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            as in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:cuts"} (1)
          %p
            We say that the Dedekind cuts form the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+completion"} Dedekind completion
            of the linear order
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Being a member of the lower (resp. the upper) set of a Dedekind cut is almost
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+proposition"} stable
              (under
              = succeed "):" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double negation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              it holds that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in L
              iff there exists a rational number
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \gt a
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg(b \in L)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in L
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg(b \in L)
              by (8), which is a contradiction to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg(b \in L)
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Equality of Dedekind cuts is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+proposition"} stable
              (under
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double negation
          %h2#variations Variations
          %p
            A Dedekind cut is, in full clarity, a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong bounded, open, rounded, located, two-sided Dedekind cut of rational numbers
          %h3#overdetermined_cuts Overdetermined cuts
          %p
            The conditions (5&amp;6) are not really necessary. If you leave them out, then the result that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &lt;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+relation"} connected relation
            on Dedekind cuts fails; but instead we identify cuts
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            whenever
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y \leq x
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+set"} quotient set
            of the set of cuts. In a similar vein, we can weaken (3,4,7) as follows:
          %ul
            %li
              3′: If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
              are rational numbers with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a' \in L
              for some
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a' \lt a
            %li
              4′: If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
              are rational numbers with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b' \in U
              for some
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ′
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b' \gt b
            %li
              7′: If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
              are rational numbers, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a' \in L
              for some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a' \lt a
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b' \in U
              for some
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ′
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b' \gt b
          %p
            Just as (3,4) follow from (7,8), so (3′,4′) follow from (7′,8). Again,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &lt;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
            is no longer connected, but we still get a linear order on a quotient set. The weakest axiom system that will give the correct linear order on the quotient is (1,2,7′,8). Incidentally, you can also recover the original definition using (3,4,7′) in place of (7).
          %p
            To emphasise that one does require (5,6), one may speak of an
            = succeed "." do
              %strong open cut
            = succeed "." do
              %strong rounded cut
          %p
            Note that we may elegantly (although requiring a fancier
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
          %ul
            %li
              (3&amp;5): A rational number
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              belongs to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              if and only if there exists a rational number
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in L
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
            %li
              (4&amp;6): A rational number
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              belongs to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              if and only if there exists a rational number
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in U
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
          %h3#covering_cuts Covering cuts
          %p
            Another approach is to
            %em strengthen
            (7):
          %ul
            %li
              7″: Every rational number belongs to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %p
            While (7″) alone does not imply (7), it does together with (8). Also note that, using (8), we can even change ‘or’ to ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/xor"}> xor
            ’ in (7″).
          %p
            The cuts that satisfy (1–6, 7″, 8) are precisely the cuts that define
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irrational+number"} irrational number
            %em both
            (5) and (6), then we get two cuts for each rational number and must (as in the previous section) pass to a quotient; this was actually Dedekind's original 1872 system. These versions are not acceptable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructively
            (and we cannot even prove that they are closed under addition), but they work fine if you use
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limited+principle+of+omniscience"} limited principle of omniscience
            seems to suffice.)
          %h3#onesided_cuts One-sided cuts
          %p
            Suppose that we consider only
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and demand (1,3,5), the axioms that refer only to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            of rational numbers is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong lower cut
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            that satisfies (2,4,6) is an
            = succeed "." do
              %strong upper cut
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+subset"} proper subset
            %strong bounded
            lower/upper cut. If we wish to emphasise that we are discussing a cut as defined above, with both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "." do
              %strong two-sided cut
          %p
            Assuming
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          #eq:twosiding.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi U
                          %mo ≔
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi ℚ
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo ∀
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi L
                          %mo ,
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi a
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo ,
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi L
                          %mo ≔
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi ℚ
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo ∀
                          %mi b
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi U
                          %mo ,
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi a
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin {split}
                  U \coloneqq \{ b\colon \mathbb{Q} \;|\; \forall a \in L,\; a \lt b \} ,\\
                  L \coloneqq \{ a\colon \mathbb{Q} \;|\; \forall b \in U,\; a \lt b \}
                  \.\end {split}
          %p
            In addition, the unbounded lower cut
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \coloneqq \mathbb{Q}
            represents
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \coloneqq \mathbb{Q}
            represents
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\infty
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lower+real"} lower real
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/upper+real"} upper real
            (with the same direction as the cut). Unlike the covering cuts in the previous section, these are actually of interest constructively. If we wish to emphasise or clarify that we are discussing a two-sided cut as in our original definition, rather than a one-sided cut which has been made two-sided through
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:twosiding"} (2)
            (which may not satisfy (7) constructively), then we may speak of a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong located cut
          %h3#unbounded_cuts Unbounded cuts
          %p
            If we drop conditions (1&amp;2), then the result is an
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong extended cut
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+real+number"} extended real number
            = succeed "." do
              %strong bounded cut
          %p
            By
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{Q},\empty)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\empty,\mathbb{Q})
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ±
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pm\infty
          %h3#intervals Interval cuts
          %p
            If we drop (7) (but keep 3&amp;4), then the result is an
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+cut"} interval cut
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [x,y]
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is a lower real and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            is an upper real. If we use only (1–6), then we allow for
            %strong back-to-front intervals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [x,y]
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y
            is no longer required. Even classically, we cannot represent back-to-front intervals as subsets of the real line, but it is still possible to compute with them.
          %p
            Whereas a located Dedekind cut can be approximated as closely as we wish, an interval cut represents a number as we measure it in the real world, where we may not be technically able (or even theoretically able, as in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
          %p
            An article on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+arithmetic"} interval arithmetic
            is probably the right place to talk about these systems (although there are many varieties of interval arithmetic).
          %h3#predicative_cuts Predicative cuts
          %p
            In predicative mathematics, subsets are large and therefore are proper classes. Instead, in the manner of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+topology"} formal topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℚ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L, U: \mathbb{Q} \to \Sigma
            representing the open subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            to define
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                \-Dedekind cuts
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is a small
            = succeed "-locale," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
